1. CREATING A SET: WHICH OF THESE IS {'a', 'b', 'c'}?
   set('abc')               yes  set {'a', 'b', 'c'}
   set(['a', 'b', 'c'])     yes  set {'a', 'b', 'c'}
   set('a', 'b', 'c')       no   TypeError: set expected at most 1 argument, got 3
   {'a', 'b', 'c'}          yes  set {'a', 'b', 'c'}
   {('a', 'b', 'c')}        no   set {('a', 'b', 'c')}
   set(x) takes ONE iterable and adds each thing it yields;
   braces take each thing written between them, whole.
   set('foo') = {'f', 'o'}      {'foo'} = {'foo'}
   type({}) = dict         type(set()) = set      the empty set has no literal
   built by add(), one at a time:  {'CP0140.1', 'EF3616.3', 'XJ8113.5'}
   comprehension {2*x for x in [1, 2, 3]} = {2, 4, 6}

2. MEMBERSHIP IS DECIDED BY hash AND ==
   {True, 1, 1.0, '1', (1,)} has 3 members, not 5:
   True == 1 == 1.0 is True, and hash(True) == hash(1) == hash(1.0) is True
   so the three are one member; the first one written is the one kept.
   {[1, 2]}               -> TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'
   {tuple([1, 2, 1])}     -> set {(1, 2, 1)}
   {frozenset({1, 2})}    -> set {frozenset({1, 2})}
   {(1, [2])}             -> TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'
   s1.add(s2)             -> TypeError: unhashable type: 'set'
   s1.update(s2)          -> s1 is now {'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'}
   s1.add(frozenset(s2))  -> 4 members: 3 letters and 1 frozenset
   add() puts in ONE member; update() puts in every member of an iterable.
   A set can hold a frozenset, never a set: a set can change, so its
   hash could change after it was filed, and it would be lost.

3. OPERATOR OR METHOD: THE SAME ANSWER, DIFFERENT MANNERS
   a = {1, 2, 3, 4}   b = {3, 4, 5}
   operation             operator  method                     result
   union                 a | b     a.union(b)                 {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
   intersection          a & b     a.intersection(b)          {3, 4}
   difference            a - b     a.difference(b)            {1, 2}
   symmetric difference  a ^ b     a.symmetric_difference(b)  {1, 2, 5}
   subset                a <= b    a.issubset(b)              False
   superset              a >= b    a.issuperset(b)            False
   proper subset         a < b     (none)                     False
   disjoint              (none)    a.isdisjoint(b)            False
   membership            3 in a    a.__contains__(3)          True
   Every row with both forms gives the same result for two sets.
   In words, with engineers = {'ann', 'bob', 'sue', 'vic'}, managers = {'sue', 'tom'}:
     'bob' in engineers                               True                                 is bob an engineer?
     engineers & managers                             {'sue'}                              who is both?
     engineers | managers                             {'ann', 'bob', 'sue', 'tom', 'vic'}  who is either?
     engineers - managers                             {'ann', 'bob', 'vic'}                engineers who are not managers
     managers - engineers                             {'tom'}                              managers who are not engineers
     engineers >= managers                            False                                is every manager an engineer?
     {'bob', 'sue'} < engineers                       True                                 are both engineers, and not all of them?
     engineers ^ managers                             {'ann', 'bob', 'tom', 'vic'}         who is in exactly one of the two?
     (engineers | managers) - (engineers ^ managers)  {'sue'}                              either, minus exactly one: both
   The difference is what they accept. A tuple on the right:
     a | t                        -> TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for |: 'set' and 'tuple'
     a.union(t)                   -> set {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
     a <= t                       -> TypeError: '<=' not supported between instances of 'set' and 'tuple'
     a.issubset(t)                -> False
     a.union([9], 'x', range(2))  -> set {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 9, 'x'}
   Operators need a set (or frozenset) on both sides; methods take any
   iterable, and several of them at once.

4. CLAIMS FROM THE NOTES, CHECKED
   claim:  'in' works but .__contains__() cannot be used
           x1.__contains__('a') = True: it is what 'in' calls. False.
   claim:  '-' has no corresponding method
           x1.difference({'a'}) = {'b', 'c'}. False.
   claim:  issubset and issuperset have no operator (N/A)
           <= and >= are those operators; {'a'} <= x1 is True. False.
   claim:  isdisjoint has no operator
           True; the nearest is 'not (a & b)', which builds a set first.
   claim:  the + operator is not supported for sets
           TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'set' and 'set'. True; use |.
   claim:  s1.add(s2) fails, use update()
           True, section 2: a set is unhashable.
   claim:  an empty set is falsy
           bool(set()) = False. True.
   claim:  issubset() of an empty iterable returns False
           set().issubset([]) = True; only a non-empty set gives False. False.
   claim:  {0,1,2,3,4} & {0,2,3,4} & {0,2,5}: the only common element is {2}
           it is {0, 2}; 0 is in all three. False.
   claim:  union takes several sets at once, and so does symmetric_difference
           a.union(b, c) works; TypeError: set.symmetric_difference() takes exactly one argument (2 given). Half true.

5. CHAINING: LEFT TO RIGHT, AND WHAT ^ OF THREE MEANS
   s1 = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}   s2 = {2, 3, 4}   s3 = {2, 5}
   s1 | s2 | s3 = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
   s1 & s2 & s3 = {2}
   s1 ^ s2      = {0, 1}
   s1 ^ s2 ^ s3 = {0, 1, 2, 5}   = (s1 ^ s2) ^ s3 = {0, 1} ^ {2, 5}
   in how many of the three sets each element is:
     0:1  1:1  2:3  3:2  4:2  5:1
   elements in an odd number of them:  {0, 1, 2, 5}   same set: True
   So ^ over many sets keeps what appears an odd number of times,
   not 'what is in exactly one'. 2 is in all three and survives.
   s1 - s2 - s3 = {0, 1}  = (s1 - s2) - s3;  s1 - (s2 - s3) = {0, 1, 2}
   | & ^ are associative, so grouping never matters for them; - is not.
   s4 = s2 | s1 | s3;  s4 |= {11, 22}  ->  {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 11, 22}
   Every operator has an in-place form: |= &= -= ^= change the left set.

6. EQUALITY: == ASKS ABOUT MEMBERS, AND NOTHING ELSE DOES
   p = {1, 9}   q = {9, 1}   list(p) = [1, 9]   list(q) = [9, 1]
   p == q                          True      the right test
   list(p) == list(q)              False     order of iteration is not membership
   {1}.difference({1, 2}) == set() True      only says {1} <= {1, 2}
   {1} == {1, 2}                  False
   not (p ^ q)                     True      also right: no member on one side only
   Equality of sets is extensionality run as code: same members, equal.
   Iteration order depends on how the set was built, so comparing lists
   of two equal sets can say False; a one-sided difference checks only <=.

7. FROZENSET: THE SET THAT CANNOT CHANGE, SO IT CAN BE A MEMBER
   fs.add(3)          -> AttributeError: 'frozenset' object has no attribute 'add'
   fs.union([3, 4])   -> frozenset {1, 2, 3, 4}   a new one; fs is unchanged
   {1} | frozenset({2}) -> set;   frozenset({2}) | {1} -> frozenset   the left operand decides
   frozenset({1, 2}) == {1, 2}  True   equality is still only about members

8. SUBSET, PROPER SUBSET, DISJOINT, EMPTY
   s <= {1, 2}                True
   s < {1, 2}                 False
   s < {1, 2, 3}              True
   set() <= s                 True
   set() < set()              False
   s.isdisjoint({3, 4})       True
   s.isdisjoint({2, 3})       False
   set().isdisjoint(set())    True
   Every set is a subset of itself, but not a proper one. The empty set
   is a subset of every set, and disjoint from every set, itself included.
   if (s <= t, u):   runs, because (s <= t, u) = (True, {5}) is a non-empty tuple
   s <= t and s <= u = False   is what was meant

9. REMOVING: WHICH ONES RAISE
   s.remove(9)      -> KeyError: 9
   s.discard(9)     -> None
   set().pop()      -> KeyError: 'pop from an empty set'
   remove() insists the member is there; discard() does not care.
   pop() takes an arbitrary member: a set has no 'first' to take.

10. ORDER IS NOT PART OF A SET
   list(set([1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5])) = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]   looks sorted, by luck:
   list(set([10, 3, 8]))      = [8, 10, 3]   small ints land in slot n % table size
   words              = ['pear', 'fig', 'apple', 'fig', 'pear', 'kiwi']
   sorted(set(words)) = ['apple', 'fig', 'kiwi', 'pear']   a list: sets cannot be sorted
   list(dict.fromkeys(words)) = ['pear', 'fig', 'apple', 'kiwi']
   To drop duplicates AND keep first-seen order, use a dict: its keys
   are a set that remembers insertion order. list(set(words)) comes back
   in hash order, which for strings changes from one run to the next.
   sorted({3, 1, 'apple', ...})  -> TypeError: '<' between an int and a str
   key=lambda x: (isinstance(x, str), x)  -> [1, 3, 5, 'apple', 'banana', 'orange']
   numbers first, since False < True, and no int is compared with a str.

11. WHAT SETS ARE FOR: MEMBERSHIP, DEDUPLICATION, VISITED NODES
   graph with cycles A->C->A and B->D->B:  {'A': ['B', 'C'], 'B': ['D'], 'C': ['D', 'A'], 'D': ['B', 'E'], 'E': []}
   breadth-first from A, skipping nodes already in 'visited': A -> B -> C -> D -> E
   Without the set the walk loops forever. With a list, each 'in' test
   scans the list; with a set it is one hash lookup, whatever the size.
   customers = {'C001', 'C002', 'C003', 'C004'}   invoiced = {'C001', 'C002', 'C004', 'C009'}
   inner join keys     customers & invoiced = {'C001', 'C002', 'C004'}
   outer join keys     customers | invoiced = {'C001', 'C002', 'C003', 'C004', 'C009'}
   left anti-join      customers - invoiced = {'C003'}   never invoiced
   orphans             invoiced - customers = {'C009'}   no such customer
   mismatches          customers ^ invoiced = {'C003', 'C009'}
   The keys a join keeps are a set operation on the two key columns;
   pandas' merge(how='inner'/'outer'/'left') is the same algebra with rows attached.

12. KATAS, WITH ANSWERS
   s1 = {'a', 'b', 'c'}   s2 = {'c', 'd', 'e'}
   K1  s1.union(s2) = {'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'}   s1 | s2 = {'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'}
   K2  s1.intersection(s2) = {'c'}   s1 & s2 = {'c'}
   K3  Venn regions:  only s1 {'a', 'b'}   both {'c'}   only s2 {'d', 'e'}
       union = all three = {'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'}
       symmetric difference = the two outer regions = {'a', 'b', 'd', 'e'} = s1 ^ s2: True
   K4  set((1, 2, 3)) & set((2, 3, 4)) & set((3, 4, 5)) = {3}
   K5  {1, 2, 3} ^ {3, 4, 5} ^ {5, 6, 7} ^ {7, 8, 1} = {2, 4, 6, 8}
       1, 3, 5 and 7 are each in two sets, an even count, so they go;
       2, 4, 6 and 8 are each in one, so they stay.
